					<div class="chapter-content">
						<!-- PART IV -->
						<div class="overview-part-block">
							<h3 class="overview-part-title">Part IV: The Science of Acid Pain Circuits</h3>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-28">Chapter 11: Acid-Sensing Ion Channels</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Your mouth is packed with specialized microscopic pain antennas called ASIC and TRPV1 channels, built specifically to scream when acidity drops below neutral pH.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">When bacteria ferment food in your gum pockets or you drink an acidic beverage, a flood of hydrogen ions locks these channels open, sending continuous burning signals.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">This proves your burning is not phantom or psychosomatic: it is a measurable electrochemical reaction to real protons touching active nerve channels.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>ASIC1, ASIC3, and TRPV1 receptor gating: the physical switches of oral burning</li>
										<li>How a drop in pH below 6.5 triggers continuous sensory neuron depolarization</li>
										<li>Proving your pain is biophysical: why hydrogen ions are tangible, measurable inputs</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-32">Chapter 12: The Oral Neuropathic Circuit</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">A leaking crown on one molar does not just make that single tooth ache; it fires continuous signals into your brainstem switchboard until your entire tongue feels on fire.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">This process, called central sensitization, tricks your brain into feeling widespread burning across the tongue and roof of your mouth even when the leak is on one tooth.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">When you seal the peripheral hardware leak at the source, the brainstem circuit stops receiving alarm signals and finally quiets down.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>Trigeminal nerve anatomy: how lingual and alveolar branches cross-talk</li>
										<li>Central sensitization: how a localized tooth leak refers burning across the whole tongue</li>
										<li>Breaking the feedback loop: quieting the brainstem by stopping the peripheral input</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-36">Chapter 13: Saliva Buffering Sleep</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Ever notice that your mouth feels relatively calm during meals, but burns like a blowtorch at three in the morning or right when you wake up?</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">During the day, your saliva constantly produces bicarbonate to neutralize acid, but at night, your salivary glands virtually turn off, leaving your teeth and gums defenseless.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Learning how to shield your margins and buffer nocturnal oral acidity is the missing key that allows tissue to heal while you sleep.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>Bicarbonate buffering chemistry: saliva as your natural fire extinguisher</li>
										<li>The midnight acid spike: why dry mouth during sleep creates morning agony</li>
										<li>Practical night buffering strategies to protect exposed root necks overnight</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>

						<!-- PART V -->
						<div class="overview-part-block">
							<h3 class="overview-part-title">Part V: Calming the Circuit</h3>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-40">Chapter 14: Starving the Fire</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Starving the fire means cutting off the fuel supply to the anaerobic bacteria hiding in your gum margins that brew lactic acid out of everyday carbs.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">When you switch to a zero-starch, zero-sugar baseline, you stop bacterial acid production at the root within forty-eight hours.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Patients who try this simple dietary pivot often see their baseline pain level drop noticeably in just three to five days.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>How oral bacteria convert bread, crackers, and fruit sugars into corrosive lactic acid</li>
										<li>The clean protein and fat baseline: starving bacterial biofilms without starving yourself</li>
										<li>The rapid 3-to-5 day drop in local subgingival acidity and mucosal irritation</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-43">Chapter 15: Mechanical Shields</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Dietary elimination removes the acid fuel, but if you have open tooth tubules or leaking margins, saliva and friction will still keep the nerve agitated.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Applying physical desensitizers like Gluma or adhesive resin bonding seals the microscopic open pipes, delivering focal relief in up to 70% to 80% of confirmed margin leaks.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Once you seal the open door, hydraulic fluid stops shifting, breaking the circuit that has been terrorizing your tongue.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>Gluma and biomimetic resin bonding: physically plugging the 40,000 open micro-pipes (70-80% focal relief)</li>
										<li>Custom nocturnal barrier trays: shielding vulnerable teeth from resting saliva and acid</li>
										<li>Hardware repair priorities: re-cementing leaking crowns and smoothing abfraction notches</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-46">Chapter 16: The Recovery Protocol</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Recovery is not random; it is an organized, step-by-step engineering sequence that takes you from total agony to lasting calm over eight structured weeks.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">We start by extinguishing the chemical fire, proceed to auditing and sealing the dental hardware, and finish by rehabilitating your oral nerves and reintroducing foods.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Having a clear roadmap replaces the overwhelming daily dread with a structured checklist you can execute with confidence.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>Phase 1 (Weeks 1-2): The zero-acid baseline and chemical fire extinguishment</li>
										<li>Phase 2 (Weeks 3-4): The hardware audit, margin cold testing, and adhesive sealing</li>
										<li>Phase 3 (Weeks 5-8): Small fiber neuro-regeneration, nocturnal buffering, and systematic food challenges</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>

						<!-- PART VI -->
						<div class="overview-part-block">
							<h3 class="overview-part-title">Part VI: Building Your Care Team</h3>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-50">Chapter 17: Finding the Right Specialists</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">You do not need to convince an uninterested dentist who dismisses your pain; you need to assemble a small team of clinicians who understand modern biomaterials and nerve biology.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">This chapter teaches you how to find biomimetic dentists who specialize in adhesive sealing, airway-literate practitioners, and compassionate neurologists.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Knowing exactly what questions to ask on the phone before booking will save you thousands of dollars and months of wasted appointments.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>How to locate biomimetic dentists trained in adhesive tubule sealing</li>
										<li>Vetting orofacial pain specialists: how to tell drug-pushers from true diagnosticians</li>
										<li>Phone screening scripts to filter out dismissive clinics before you pay for an exam</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-53">Chapter 18: Doctor Dentist Clinic Guide</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">If you walk into an appointment crying and talking about mysterious burning, doctors often reach for psychiatric prescriptions or label you as anxious.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">When you bring a precise 72-hour food ledger, receptor biophysics, and specific requests for margin cold-testing, their entire attitude shifts to clinical respect.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Use our word-for-word conversation scripts to get your dentist to look under crown margins and your doctor to order functional deficiency blood panels.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>Word-for-word scripts for dental exams: requesting margin micro-checks without offending the dentist</li>
										<li>Presenting your 72-hour time lag ledger as clinical engineering data</li>
										<li>Getting the right blood panels ordered: ferritin under 50 ng/mL, active B12, and serum zinc</li>
									</ol>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>

						<!-- PART VII -->
						<div class="overview-part-block">
							<h3 class="overview-part-title">Part VII: For Medical Professionals</h3>

							<div class="overview-chapter-row">
								<div class="overview-main">
									<h4 class="overview-item-title"><a href="#page-56">Chapter 19: The Institutional Divide</a></h4>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Modern medicine cut the human head in half: dentists handle teeth and bone, doctors handle nerves and organs, and neither profession studies where hardware meets saliva biochemistry.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Patients with Burning Mouth Syndrome fall directly into this institutional crevasse, suffering for years while specialists point fingers at each other across the hall.</p>
									<p class="overview-sentence">Understanding this administrative failure helps you realize that your condition was never an incurable enigma: it was simply an orphan of medical specialization.</p>
								</div>
								<div class="overview-subtopics">
									<div class="overview-subtopics-header">Core Subtopics</div>
									<ol>
										<li>The historical turf war between dental colleges and medical universities</li>
										<li>The tragic curriculum gap: why neither doctors nor dentists learn oral sensory neuroscience</li>
										<li>Building the bridge: how simple cross-discipline testing solves chronic cases</li>
									</ol>
